 12. Customer access tokens are placed in URLs

  Tokens in query strings can appear in:

  - Browser history.
  - Access logs.
  - Analytics.
  - Error reporting.
  - Same-origin referrer headers.

  The current referrer policy limits cross-origin leakage, but logs and analytics remain relevant. Store the token in sessionStorage during checkout and send it in a
  request header/body instead.

  13. JWT refresh security could be tightened

  Current refresh tokens last 30 days, rotate, but old rotated tokens are not blacklisted. A stolen refresh token can remain usable.

  Recommended:

  - Enable refresh-token blacklisting.
  - Consider a shorter refresh lifetime for an admin-only system.
  - Scope the refresh cookie to /api/token/.
  - Use SameSite=Lax if all browser calls continue through the same-origin Next.js proxy.
  - CSRF/origin-protect refresh and logout endpoints.


 11. Notifications are not transactionally reliable

  Order changes commit first, then email/SMS runs synchronously. If the process stops after the commit but before notifications finish, Stripe retries the webhook.
  However, the webhook sees an already-succeeded payment and exits, so the missed notification may never be retried.

  An outbox approach would be safer:

  Webhook transaction
    ├── mark payment/order paid
    └── create NotificationJob

  Worker/command
    └── send and retry NotificationJob

  This also makes Stripe webhooks respond faster.


 2. Stop putting order bearer tokens in URLs.

  Customer access tokens currently travel through query strings in all checkout systems, for example:

  - frontend/app/checkout/[slug]/_components/CheckoutScreen.tsx:100
  - frontend/app/parts/checkout/_components/PartsCheckoutDetailsScreen.tsx:53
  - frontend/lib/api.ts:339

  These tokens can enter browser history, server/proxy logs, referrers, analytics, screenshots, and support messages.

  The strongest solution is to exchange the token once for a short-lived, scoped HttpOnly order cookie. A simpler improvement is sending it in an
  authorization header or POST body and keeping checkout state in sessionStorage, never the URL.

  3. Sanitize rendered HTML and safely serialize JSON-LD.

  Two meaningful XSS surfaces exist:

  - Backend-provided terms HTML is inserted directly in frontend/app/terms/page.tsx:51.
  - Markdown is converted by marked and rendered directly in frontend/app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx:120.